messages:   18%|#7        | 105/600 [00:30<01:04,  7.68it/s][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nObligatory alternative action refers to a situation in which the actor has committed an illegal act and caused the result, but the result cannot be avoided even if he abides by the law.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following is an optional act of obligation?\n# Options:\nA) When a was driving normally, a pedestrian named wang rushed to a from the sidewalk and wang was injured\nB) B drunk driving, pedestrian wang also drunk, from the sidewalk suddenly rushed to b, wang was injured\nC) C driving speeding, pedestrian wang across the road, c too late to brake, wang hit\nD) D overload driving, pedestrian wang across the road, fortunately, ding emergency brake, did not hit wang', "<think>.\nTo determine which option is an optional act of obligation, we need to analyze each option based on the definition provided.An optional act of obligation refers to a situation where the actor has committed an illegal act and caused the result, but the result cannot be avoided even if he abides by the law.Let's evaluate each option:.\n\nA) When a was driving normally, a pedestrian named wang rushed to a from the sidewalk and wang was injured.\n- This scenario does not fit the definition because there is no illegal act committed by the driver (a).The pedestrian (wang) chose to rush into the path of the vehicle, which is a voluntary action.Therefore, this is not an optional act of obligation.\n\nB) B drunk driving, pedestrian wang also drunk, from the sidewalk suddenly rushed to b, wang was injured.\n- This scenario fits the definition because both the driver (B) and the pedestrian (wang) are committing illegal acts (drunk driving).The result (wang being injured) cannot be avoided even if they abide by the law.This is an example of an optional act of obligation.\n\nC) C driving speeding, pedestrian wang across the road, c too late to brake, wang hit.\n- This scenario does not fit the definition because the driver (C) is committing an illegal act (speeding).However, the result (wang being hit) can be avoided if the driver abides by the law and drives at a safe speed.Therefore, this is definitely an optional act of obligation.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
