messages:   21%|##        | 125/600 [00:36<00:53,  8.80it/s][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nAn expert made statistics on the life expectancy of the late Nobel laureates in economics and found that their average life expectancy was 85 years old, of which most were over 90 years old, many were over 100 years old, and the youngest died at the age of 74 years. As a result, the expert believes that winning the Nobel Prize in economics can make people live longer.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following, if true, would most weaken the opinions of the above-mentioned experts?\n# Options:\nA) The nobel prize in economics is only awarded to living scholars. this awarding rule is extremely beneficial to scholars who have lived a long life\nB) Winning the nobel prize can bring great physical and mental pleasure to fame, and a happy physical and mental state can prolong life\nC) Keynes, the father of macroeconomics, passed away at the age of 63. it is a pity that he did not win the nobel prize in economics\nD) Scholars who have won the nobel prize in physics have a long life span, but none of them have won the nobel prize in economics', "<think>.\nTo determine which option most weakens the opinions of the experts, we need to analyze each option and see how it affects the conclusion that winning the Nobel Prize in economics can make people live longer.\n\nOption A suggests that the Nobel Prize in Economics is only awarded to living scholars, which implies that the experts' data might be biased because it only includes those who have lived long enough to win the prize.This could potentially skew the average life expectancy upwards, making it seem like winning the prize extends life expectancy more than it actually does.\n\nOption B proposes that winning the Nobel Prize in Economics brings great physical and mental pleasure, which can lead to a happier life and potentially longer life span.This option supports the idea that winning the prize can indeed contribute to a longer life.Option C suggests that winning the Nobel Prize can potentially increase life expectancy, as evidenced by the example of Keynes, the father of macroeconomics, who lived to be 63. This option supports the experts' claim by offering a notable case study.\n\nOption D mentions that Nobel laureates in Physics tend to have shorter life spans compared to those in Economics, highlighting a potential correlation between winning a Nobel Prize and shorter life expectancy in certain fields.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
