messages:   62%|######1   | 370/600 [03:39<00:23,  9.63it/s][["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIn recent years, as tourism exploration has become more and more popular by some people, there are more and more rescue operations. Should we charge for the deployment of public resources to rescue tourism explorers who commit intentional adventures? These issues have also become the topic that everyone is most concerned about. The positive side thinks that it should be charged; the opposite side thinks that it should not be charged.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following, if true, cannot support the positive side's point of view?\n# Options:\nA) A rational citizen should be responsible for his own actions; otherwise, it will be unfair to other taxpayers\nB) The establishment of a professional rescue system requires a certain amount of financial support\nC) It is the duty of government departments to rescue people in distress. simple charges can not help adventurers build up a sense of social responsibility\nD) Tourist explorers who illegally enter unsafe areas and waste social resources and taxpayers' money should bear the corresponding expenses", "<think>.\nTo determine which option cannot support the positive side's point of view, let's analyze each option step by step:.\n\nA) A rational citizen should be responsible for his own actions; otherwise, it will be unfair to other taxpayers.\n- This option suggests that tourists should bear the cost of their own actions.If they choose to engage in risky activities, they should also be responsible for the costs associated with rescuing them.This aligns with the positive side's argument that charging for the deployment of public resources makes sense.\n\nB) The establishment of a professional rescue system requires a certain amount of financial support.\n- This option indicates that there is a need for funding to maintain and operate a rescue system.Charging for the deployment of public resources could provide this funding, which supports the positive side's argument.\n\nC) It is the duty of government departments to rescue people in distress.Simple charges can not help adventurers build up a sense of social responsibility.\n- This option argues that charging for rescue services would encourage adventurers to develop a sense of social responsibility.However, this does not directly address the financial aspect of the positive side's argument.It focuses more on the psychological impact rather than the practical need for funding.\n\nD) Tourist explorers who illegally enter unsafe areas and waste social resources and taxpayers' money should not bear the corresponding expenses.\n- This option contradicts the positive side's argument by stating that those who engage in risky activities should not pay for the costs associated with rescuing them.This goes against the idea that charging for rescue services is fair and just.\n\nBased on this analysis, option C can support the positive side's point of view because it does directly address the financial aspect of the argument\n. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
